{
  "concept": "The fluid force on a vertically submerged flat surface is built up by slicing the surface into thin horizontal strips whose nearly-constant depth makes force ≈ pressure × area, and summing them gives the Riemann sum whose limit is F = ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Fluid region background: vertical gradient from pale blue at the top to deeper blue at the bottom",
    "Wavy black line across the top of each panel representing the fluid surface (x = 0)",
    "Downward-pointing vertical depth axis on the left of each panel, arrowhead at bottom, with tick marks",
    "Irregular blob-shaped submerged plate, filled pale orange with a heavy black outline, identical shape in all three panels",
    "Panel (a): red dashed horizontal lines at depths a (top of plate) and b (bottom of plate), with axis labels 'a' and 'b'",
    "Panel (a): tick and label 'x' at an intermediate depth on the axis",
    "Panel (a): horizontal light-blue chord across the plate at depth x with solid black dots at its two endpoints, labeled 'w(x)' above the chord",
    "Panel (a): double-headed vertical arrow at the right from the fluid surface down to level x, labeled 'h(x)'",
    "Panel (b): red dashed partition lines at depths a = x₀, x₁, x₂, x₃, …, x_{n-1}, b = xₙ, each extending from the axis to the plate",
    "Panel (b): light-blue chords across the plate at each interior partition depth, cutting the plate into horizontal strips",
    "Panel (b): strip labels A₁, A₂, A₃, …, Aₙ placed inside their strips",
    "Panel (b): vertical ellipsis dots on the axis and inside the plate indicating omitted strips",
    "Panel (c): close-up of one strip — axis labels a, x_{k-1}, x*_k, x_k, b with a solid dot at x*_k",
    "Panel (c): red dashed line at depth x*_k running across the plate, with black dots at the two edge crossings",
    "Panel (c): approximating rectangle drawn over the strip, filled pale green, of width w(x*_k) and height Δx_k",
    "Panel (c): vertical double-headed arrow at the right of the rectangle labeled 'Δx_k'",
    "Panel (c): vertical double-headed arrow from the fluid surface down to x*_k labeled 'h(x*_k)'",
    "Panel (c): horizontal double-headed arrow with end bars beneath the plate labeled 'w(x*_k)'",
    "Panel labels '(a)', '(b)', '(c)' centered beneath each panel",
    "Readout box (interactive): current values of h(x*_k), w(x*_k), Δx_k, strip force ρ·h·w·Δx",
    "Running-total bar/readout (interactive): partial sum Σ ρ h(x*_k) w(x*_k) Δx_k compared with the exact integral value"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "Single animated panel"],
      "default": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)",
      "teaches": "That the three textbook panels are three stages of one idea — whole plate, partition, single strip — and can be collapsed into one evolving picture."
    },
    {
      "id": "numStrips",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of strips n",
      "range": [2, 60, 1],
      "default": 6,
      "teaches": "As the partition is refined, each strip's depth varies less, and the Riemann sum of strip forces converges to the exact integral value."
    },
    {
      "id": "stripIndex",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Strip index k",
      "range": [1, 60, 1],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "Each strip contributes ρ·h(x*_k)·w(x*_k)·Δx_k — deeper strips carry more pressure, wider strips more area, so the contribution depends on both."
    },
    {
      "id": "accumulate",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Accumulate strip forces top → bottom",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "The total force is literally built up strip by strip; the running sum grows fastest where the plate is both wide and deep.",
      "states": ["idle", "running", "done"],
      "default": "idle"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The whole submerged plate",
      "narration": "A flat plate hangs vertically in fluid between depths a and b. At any depth x the plate has width w(x) and lies h(x) below the surface — pressure is not constant over the plate, so force ≠ pressure × area.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "numStrips": 1, "accumulate": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slice into horizontal strips",
      "narration": "Partition the depth interval a = x₀ < x₁ < … < xₙ = b. Each strip A_k is thin enough that every point on it sits at nearly the same depth, so pressure is nearly constant across it.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "numStrips": 6, "stripIndex": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Zoom in on one strip",
      "narration": "Drag the k slider: the highlighted strip is approximated by a rectangle of width w(x*_k) and height Δx_k at depth h(x*_k). Its force is ρ·h(x*_k)·w(x*_k)·Δx_k — watch the readout change as you move deeper or into a wider part of the plate.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "numStrips": 6, "stripIndex": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Add up all the strips",
      "narration": "Play the accumulation: the strip forces are added top to bottom, giving the Riemann sum Σ ρ h(x*_k) w(x*_k) Δx_k shown against the exact value.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "numStrips": 6, "accumulate": "running" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Refine the partition",
      "narration": "Increase n. The rectangles hug the plate more closely and the sum converges to F = ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx — the definite integral is the limit of exactly this sum.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "numStrips": 40, "accumulate": "done" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Pressure varies with depth, so force can't be computed in one shot — but on a strip thin enough that depth is essentially constant, force = ρ·h·(area), and summing those strips and letting their thickness go to zero is precisely the integral ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three stacked panels sharing the same identity: pale-blue-to-blue vertical gradient fluid, a black wavy line for the surface at top, and a downward vertical depth axis with an arrowhead at the bottom-left. The same irregular kidney/blob-shaped plate (pale orange fill, thick black outline, a concave notch on its lower edge) appears in all three, roughly centered. Panel (a): red dashed horizontal lines at depths a and b touching the top and bottom of the blob; a labeled tick 'x' between them; a thin light-blue chord across the blob at depth x with black endpoint dots and the label w(x) above it; a vertical double-headed arrow at the right from the wavy surface to level x labeled h(x). Panel (b): red dashed lines from the axis to the blob at a = x₀, x₁, x₂, x₃, …, x_{n-1}, b = xₙ, continued inside the blob as light-blue chords; strip labels A₁, A₂, A₃ near the top strips and Aₙ near the bottom; vertical ellipses on both the axis and inside the blob for the omitted middle strips. Panel (c): a zoomed strip — axis labels a, x_{k-1}, x*_k (with a solid dot), x_k, b; a red dashed line across the blob at x*_k with black dots at the blob edges; a pale-green approximating rectangle spanning the blob's width at that depth with a thin black border; a Δx_k double arrow at its right edge; an h(x*_k) double arrow from the surface down to x*_k at the far right; a w(x*_k) horizontal double arrow with end bars below the blob. Italic serif math labels throughout; panel captions (a), (b), (c) centered underneath. Interactive additions: highlight the selected strip in gold/green with the rest dimmed, a small readout panel showing h, w, Δx and strip force, and a horizontal progress bar comparing the running Riemann sum to the exact integral value."
}

